# Gradium TypeScript Client

TypeScript/JavaScript client for the [Gradium Voice AI API](https://gradium.ai):
Text-to-Speech, Speech-to-Text and Speech-to-Speech over WebSocket, plus voice
management and usage tracking over HTTP.

[Documentation](docs/README.md) · [Quick start guide](docs/quickstart.md) ·
[SDK reference](docs/api.md)

## Install

```bash
npm install @gradium/sdk
```

Requires Node.js ≥ 18. Works in browsers too (see [Browser usage](#browser-usage)).

## Quick start

```ts
import { GradiumClient } from "@gradium/sdk";
import { writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";

const client = new GradiumClient({ apiKey: "gsk_..." }); // or GRADIUM_API_KEY env var

// Buffered TTS: get all audio at once
const result = await client.tts(
  { voice_id: "YTpq7expH9539ERJ", output_format: "wav" },
  "Hello, world!",
);
await writeFile("out.wav", result.rawData);
```

## Text-to-Speech

### Streaming

```ts
const stream = await client.ttsStream({ output_format: "pcm" }, "Hello world");
for await (const chunk of stream.iterBytes()) {
  play(chunk); // raw audio bytes as they are synthesized
}
// alignment info collected during streaming:
for (const t of stream.textWithTimestamps) {
  console.log(`${t.text}: ${t.startS}s - ${t.stopS}s`);
}
```

`stream.iterEvents()` yields all TTS messages, including `need_more_text`,
alignment, audio and `end_of_stream`. Audio is decoded to `Uint8Array`.
Each stream has a single consumer: choose one iterator per stream.

With `"pcm"` output the audio is 48 kHz, 16-bit signed mono. Other formats:
`"wav"`, `"opus"`, `"ulaw_8000"`, `"alaw_8000"`, `"pcm_8000"`, `"pcm_16000"`,
`"pcm_24000"`.

### Realtime (interactive)

Send text incrementally — e.g. as an LLM generates it — while audio streams
back concurrently:

```ts
const tts = await client.ttsRealtime({ voice_id: "..." }).connect();

for (const word of words) await tts.sendText(word);
await tts.sendEos();

for await (const msg of tts) {
  if (msg.type === "audio")
    play(msg.audio); // Uint8Array
  else if (msg.type === "end_of_stream") break;
}
tts.close();
```

### Multiple requests on one connection

Use a multiplexed session to reuse a WebSocket across independent requests.
Every setup, text and EOS message carries a request ID, and each setup must set
`close_ws_on_eos: false`:

```ts
const session = await client.ttsMultiplex().connect({ timeoutMs: 30_000 });
const requests = [
  { id: "first", text: "Hello!" },
  { id: "second", text: "Another request on the same connection." },
];
const sending = (async () => {
  for (const { id, text } of requests) {
    await session.sendSetup({
      voice_id: "YTpq7expH9539ERJ",
      output_format: "pcm",
      client_req_id: id,
      close_ws_on_eos: false,
    });
    await session.sendText(text, id);
    await session.sendEos(id);
  }
})();

try {
  const completed = new Set<string>();
  for await (const msg of session) {
    if (msg.type === "audio") playForRequest(msg.client_req_id, msg.audio);
    if (msg.type === "error") console.error(msg.client_req_id, msg.message);
    if ((msg.type === "end_of_stream" || msg.type === "error") && msg.client_req_id) {
      completed.add(msg.client_req_id);
      if (completed.size === requests.length) break;
    }
  }
} finally {
  session.close();
  await sending;
}
```

Errors carrying a `client_req_id` are yielded as events so other requests can
continue. Connection errors fail the session. Each request's EOS is yielded;
the connection remains open until closed, cancelled or disconnected.

## Speech-to-Text

### Buffered

```ts
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";

const audio = new Uint8Array(await readFile("audio.wav"));
const result = await client.stt({ input_format: "wav", json_config: { language: "en" } }, audio);
console.log(result.text);
```

Buffered results apply final `end_text` timestamps. Streaming `iterText()` yields
segments immediately and updates `stopS` on the most recent segment for the
matching stream when `end_text` arrives. `iterEvents()` exposes the original
events when you need to process finalization yourself. STT streams expose
`sampleRate` and `frameSize` from the server's ready message.

Audio input can be:

- `Uint8Array` / `ArrayBuffer` — encoded bytes in the setup's `input_format`
- `Int16Array` — PCM samples (24 kHz mono, `input_format: "pcm"`)
- `Float32Array` — PCM samples in [-1, 1], converted to int16 automatically
- `AsyncIterable` of any of the above — streamed as-is

### Realtime with VAD

```ts
const stt = await client
  .sttRealtime({ input_format: "pcm", json_config: { language: "en" } })
  .connect();

// send 80ms chunks (1920 samples at 24kHz)
micStream.on("data", (chunk) => stt.sendAudio(chunk));

for await (const msg of stt) {
  if (msg.type === "text") {
    console.log("transcript:", msg.text);
  } else if (msg.type === "step") {
    // VAD every 80ms — recommended turn-taking check:
    const turnEnded = msg.vad[2].inactivity_prob > 0.5;
  }
}
```

`stt.sendFlush(id)` forces buffered audio to be transcribed immediately (the
server replies with a `flushed` message).

## Speech-to-Speech

Pipe audio through recognition, optional translation, and synthesis:

```ts
const result = await client.s2s(
  {
    model_name: "s2s-translate", // currently the only supported S2S model
    stt_model_name: "stt-translate",
    tts_model_name: "default",
    input_format: "wav",
    output_format: "wav",
    voice_id: "...", // required — without it the pipeline silently produces no output
    json_config: { target_language: "en" }, // omit to keep the original language
  },
  new Uint8Array(await readFile("speech.wav")),
);
console.log(result.text); // translated transcript
await writeFile("out.wav", result.rawData); // synthesized audio
```

Streaming (`client.s2sStream`) and realtime (`client.s2sRealtime`) variants
work like their TTS/STT counterparts.

## Voices

```ts
const voices = await client.voiceList();
const voice = await client.voiceCreate(await readFile("speaker.wav"), {
  language: "en",
  name: "My Voice",
  filename: "speaker.wav",
  description: "A warm, friendly voice",
  startS: 0.5, // skip leading silence
});
await client.voiceUpdate(voice.uid, { name: "Renamed" });
await client.voiceDelete(voice.uid);
```

### Voice designer

Generate candidate voices from a description, preview them, and save a chosen
draft as a permanent voice:

```ts
const { embeddings } = await client.voiceGenerate("A warm, calm narrator", {
  language: "en",
  nSamples: 2, // 1–5 candidates
  wait: true,
  timeoutMs: 120_000,
});
const draft = embeddings[0]!;
const preview = await client.voiceTts(draft.embedding_id, "Hello, world!");
await writeFile("preview.wav", preview.rawData);
const voice = await client.voiceFromEmbedding(draft.embedding_id, {
  name: "My narrator",
});
```

Without `wait: true`, generation returns queued drafts immediately. Check their
status with `client.voiceEmbeddingGet(id)`. Waiting defaults to a total 120-second
deadline and a 1000ms poll interval; customize `timeoutMs` and `pollIntervalMs`.
Drafts expire unless saved. Preview returns a `TTSResult`; the HTTP endpoint
does not provide a request ID, sample rate or alignment timestamps.

## Usage & credits

```ts
const { remaining, allocated } = await client.credits();
const summary = await client.usageSummary();
```

## Browser usage

Browsers cannot set headers on WebSocket connections, so the client
authenticates with a temporary token as a `?token=` query parameter. This is
handled automatically (a fresh token is fetched via `api-keys/token` before each
connection), or you can supply one fetched by your backend:

```ts
const client = new GradiumClient({ token });
```

> Avoid shipping long-lived API keys to browsers — mint temporary tokens on
> your server with `client.tempToken()` and hand those to the frontend.

Supplied tokens also work with Node and custom WebSocket factories. HTTP
operations, including voice management and token creation, require an API key.
Create a new client with a fresh supplied token when it expires.

## Cancellation, deadlines and cleanup

Speech helpers accept `{ signal, timeoutMs }` as their final argument. Realtime
and multiplexed sessions accept these options in `connect()`:

```ts
const controller = new AbortController();
const stream = await client.ttsStream({ output_format: "pcm" }, textSource, {
  signal: controller.signal,
  timeoutMs: 30_000,
});
try {
  for await (const chunk of stream.iterBytes()) play(chunk);
} finally {
  await stream.close();
}
// To interrupt setup, sending or receiving: controller.abort();
```

The deadline covers connection, setup, input and output. `timeoutMs` must be a
positive finite number; speech operations have no deadline unless one is set.
Voice designer helpers and low-level HTTP methods also accept cancellation and
deadline options.

Single-request streams complete when the server sends EOS, even if the socket
stays open. Breaking an iterator closes its session. Explicitly close a stream
you create but do not consume; streams and realtime sessions also support
`Symbol.asyncDispose` for environments using `await using`.

Cancellation stops consuming input and requests iterator cleanup without
waiting for a blocked producer. Give your microphone or LLM source the same
signal so it can release its own resources and cancel a pending read.

## Error handling

- `GradiumAPIError` — non-2xx HTTP response (`.status`, `.body`)
- `GradiumWebSocketError` — server `error` message or abnormal close (`.code`;
  `1008` policy violation, `1011` internal server error)
- Cancellation rejects with the signal's reason (normally `AbortError`);
  deadlines reject with `TimeoutError`.

